The column is "28 day range", so the likely formula is the maximum minus the minimum of the 28-day results in Q6, R6 and S6.

S6 holds "-", which is text. MAX and MIN ignore text in references, so including S6 is safe.

{"formula": "=MAX(Q6,R6,S6)-MIN(Q6,R6,S6)"}

The O column is headed "Error" with the unit "( OF )", which suggests an objective function. The columns are P eksp in E (cmHg), Peksp in G (kPa), and P calc in N (kPa). The error is most likely the squared difference between the experimental and calculated pressure, so the formula compares G13 with N13.

An objective function (OF) in VLE data regression is commonly written as (Pexp − Pcalc)², or as ((Pexp − Pcalc)/Pexp)². The ARD column in P then holds the absolute relative deviation in %. I chose the squared difference because the header is "Error ( OF )".

{"formula": "=(G13-N13)^2"}

The column is "Ave Score" and sits beside "Oscars" and "Ave Time". Those columns use AVERAGEIF on the Film sheet. Column E there holds the directors, N holds the runtime, and AI holds the score (used for Best and Worst film). So the formula should average column AI for films matching the director.

The wildcard pattern in B2, D2 and E2 is `"*"&A2&"*"`, so I'll reuse it.

{"formula": "=AVERAGEIF(Film!$E$2:$E$20000,\"*\"&A2&\"*\",Film!$AI$2:$AI$20000)"}

I'm guessing here, since the prefix is only `=`. The header in T6 is "Avg. Positions held", and the nearby columns are Beg. Positions (J) and End. Positions (P). The simplest reading is the average of the beginning and ending positions. `AVERAGE` skips text in references, so it still works when J7 shows "-".

An alternative would be averaging the monthly counts in AC for the year, but I went with the simpler one.

{"formula": "=AVERAGE(J7,P7)"}

I50 is the tons/yr column for the "Potential Emissions After Controls/Limit" pair, so it should mirror H50 times 8760/2000, as I48 does for H48. I50 is currently empty and its neighbours I51 to I53 are empty too, so the pattern comes from H50 and from the I46 to I49 formulas.

H50 is `=CHOOSE(INPUT!$F$23,INPUT!$F$22,FACTORS!$C$44)*2.012*FACTORS!$B$5*INPUT!$F$35/INPUT!$F$40`. Appending `*8760/2000` gives the I50 formula.

{"formula": "=CHOOSE(INPUT!$F$23,INPUT!$F$22,FACTORS!$C$44)*2.012*FACTORS!$B$5*INPUT!$F$35/INPUT!$F$40*8760/2000"}

Cell H13 is the count for "Other", and the pattern above it is COUNTIF over column P (H9:H12). H8 uses column N, which looks like an inconsistency. "Other" most likely means everything not already counted, so the formula would be a total minus the categories above.

Using the existing counts, one option is a total of the P column entries minus the listed ones. The simplest and most likely form follows the existing pattern: `=COUNTIF(P:P,G13)`. This matches the user's pattern, so I'll use it.

{"formula": "=COUNTIF(P:P,G13)"}

The row above counts nights with COUNTIF over B3:B33 for "N", and B48 is labeled "Day". Day shifts are marked "T" or "O" in the schedule, but the legend is partly cut off. It says " = Work", with the leading character missing. The "o = Off" line means "O" marks off days, so the work code is probably "W" or "T". The data shows N, O, T, and blanks. Since "T" is probably a transition or training code, the most likely intent is to count the "O" entries. However, the legend says "O" means Off, which is not a day shift.

The legend's missing character in " = Work" is most likely "T" or "W", and the data contains "T" but no "W". I'll go with "T" as the work code, so the day count is COUNTIF(B3:B33,"T").

{"formula": "=COUNTIF(B3:B33,\"T\")"}

