G4 holds the length of the project in months, calculated from the start date in E4 and the end date in F4. DATEDIF with "m" returns whole months, so a project from 2023-12-01 to 2024-05-31 would show 5. Because the end dates fall on the last day of the month, adding 1 to the end date gives a full-month count of 6, which is what the user most likely wants.

I chose `DATEDIF(E4,F4+1,"m")` for that reason. A simpler `YEARFRAC`-based formula would give fractional values, which seem less likely to be intended.

{"formula": "=DATEDIF(E4,F4+1,\"m\")"}

